   "id": 615938,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<figure>\n<img src=\"exercises/media/997d4f902aa4d5c4.png\" alt=\"A giant Uncle Sam heads to China, stepping on the Philippines on his way there. An American flag is planted in the Philippines. Uncle Sam holds steel rails, a book labeled education and religion, a mowing machine, trains, a sewing machine, and bridges. A Chinaman stands on the shore with his arms outstretched. Surrounding him are signs that read “Wanted: Watches, Clocks. Wanted: Wagons, carriages, trucks. Wanted: 100,000 bridges, 500,000 engines, 2,000,000 cars, 4,000,000 rails, 100,000 railroad stations. Wanted: 50,000,000 sewing machines, 50,000,000 reaping machines, 100,000,000 plows. Wanted: cotton goods, telegraph, telephone, electricity. Wanted: stoves, lamps, petroleum, medicines, chemicals, disinfectants. Wanted: trolley lines, electric light. Wanted: water-works, sewers, paving, asphalt roads.” The caption reads “And, after all, the Philippines are only the stepping-stone to China.”\">\n<figcaption>This cartoon, from around 1900, shows Uncle Sam bringing industry, education, and the Christian religion to the Philippines and then China.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><i>Refer to the image provided.</i></p>\n<p>The actions of Uncle Sam as represented in the cartoon would be opposed by</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1171721,
     "content_html": "Emilio Aguinaldo"
    },
    {
     "id": 1171720,
     "content_html": "Theodore Roosevelt"
    },
    {
     "id": 1171719,
     "content_html": "William McKinley"
    },
    {
     "id": 1171718,
     "content_html": "American industrialists"
    }
